        ("Choose the correct conjunction: The employee shall be held liable ___ he can prove that the loss was due to force majeure.", ["unless","if","because","although"], "unless", "'Unless' = 'if not' — liable unless proven otherwise.", ["unless","legal defense"]),
        ("Choose the correct conjunction: ___ the motion was filed beyond the reglementary period, it is hereby denied.", ["Inasmuch as","Notwithstanding that","In spite of the fact that","Although"], "Inasmuch as", "'Inasmuch as' = 'because/since' — the late filing is the reason for denial.", ["inasmuch as","causal","formal"]),
        ("Choose the correct conjunction: The committee resolved to defer action ___ additional evidence could be gathered.", ["so that","in order that","until","Both A and B are correct."], "Both A and B are correct.", "'So that' and 'in order that' both express purpose — deferring to allow evidence gathering.", ["purpose","so that","in order that"]),
        ("Choose the correct conjunction: ___ it may appear that the respondent acted in good faith, the objective evidence suggests otherwise.", ["Although","While","Whereas","All of the above are correct."], "All of the above are correct.", "All three can introduce concessive clauses in formal writing.", ["concessive","multiple correct","formal"]),
        ("Choose the correct conjunction: The employee was terminated ___ for cause ___ due to redundancy.", ["either ... or","neither ... nor","not ... but","both ... and"], "not ... but", "'Not for cause but due to redundancy' — corrects one reason and states the actual one.", ["correlative","not...but","correction"]),
        ("Choose the correct conjunction: ___ the respondent's repeated requests for extension, the committee granted a final period of fifteen days.", ["In deference to","Notwithstanding","In spite of","Because of"], "In deference to", "'In deference to' means 'out of respect for' — the committee respected the requests.", ["in deference to","formal expression"]),
        ("Choose the correct conjunction: The regulation applies to all employees ___ of rank, status, or length of service.", ["regardless","irrespective","whether","Both A and B are correct."], "Both A and B are correct.", "'Regardless of' and 'irrespective of' are synonymous formal expressions.", ["regardless/irrespective","formal","multiple correct"]),
        ("Choose the correct conjunction: ___ the complainant substantiates his allegations with competent evidence, the complaint must be dismissed.", ["Unless","If","Provided that","In the event that"], "Unless", "'Unless' = 'if not' — dismissal occurs if allegations are NOT substantiated.", ["unless","burden of proof"]),
        ("Choose the correct conjunction: The decision is immediately executory ___ a motion for reconsideration is filed.", ["unless","even if","notwithstanding that","although"], "even if", "'Even if' means the decision remains executory regardless of whether a motion is filed.", ["even if","concessive conditional"]),
        ("Choose the correct conjunction: ___ the penalty of dismissal carries with it forfeiture of benefits, the respondent is given the option to voluntarily resign.", ["Considering that","Inasmuch as","In view of the fact that","All of the above are correct."], "All of the above are correct.", "All three are formal causal conjunctions meaning 'because/since.'", ["formal causal","multiple correct"]),
        ("Choose the correct conjunction: The employee was found guilty; ___, the penalty of suspension for six months is imposed.", ["however","accordingly","nevertheless","moreover"], "accordingly", "'Accordingly' = 'therefore/as a result' — the penalty follows from the finding of guilt.", ["conjunctive adverb","result","formal"]),
        ("Choose the correct conjunction: ___ the evidence is insufficient to prove the charge of dishonesty, the respondent is found guilty of the lesser offense of simple neglect of duty.", ["While","Since","Although","Whereas"], "While", "'While' here means 'although' — concedes insufficient evidence for one charge but finds guilt on another.", ["while as concessive","legal finding"]),
        ("Choose the correct conjunction: The committee shall resolve the case ___ the submission of the parties' respective memoranda.", ["upon","after","following","All of the above are correct."], "All of the above are correct.", "'Upon,' 'after,' and 'following' all indicate the temporal trigger for resolution.", ["temporal","multiple correct","formal"]),
        ("Choose the correct conjunction: ___ the respondent invokes the defense of good faith, he must prove that he exercised due diligence.", ["If","Should","Where","All of the above are correct."], "All of the above are correct.", "'If,' 'Should' (inverted), and 'Where' (in the case that) all introduce conditions in legal writing.", ["conditional","legal","multiple correct"]),
        ("Choose the correct conjunction: The employee was exonerated ___ the complainant failed to present substantial evidence.", ["because","inasmuch as","considering that","All of the above are correct."], "All of the above are correct.", "All express causation — the failure to present evidence caused the exoneration.", ["causal","multiple correct","formal"]),
        ("Choose the correct conjunction: ___ the foregoing discussion, the committee finds no merit in the complaint.", ["Premised on","Based on","In light of","All of the above are correct."], "All of the above are correct.", "All three introduce a conclusion based on preceding discussion.", ["conclusion","formal","multiple correct"]),
        ("Choose the correct conjunction: The suspension shall be lifted ___ the employee completes the required training.", ["once","after","upon","All of the above are correct."], "All of the above are correct.", "All indicate the temporal condition for lifting the suspension.", ["temporal condition","multiple correct"]),
        ("Choose the correct conjunction: ___ the respondent was a first-time offender, the committee imposed the minimum penalty.", ["Considering that","In view of the fact that","Given that","All of the above are correct."], "All of the above are correct.", "All are formal causal conjunctions explaining why the minimum penalty was chosen.", ["mitigating circumstance","causal","formal"]),
        ("Choose the correct conjunction: The employee shall be reinstated ___ back wages ___ the period of his unjust suspension.", ["with ... covering","together with ... for","with ... for","Both A and C are correct."], "Both A and C are correct.", "'With back wages for/covering the period' — both preposition choices work.", ["with","formal expression"]),
        ("Choose the correct conjunction: ___ the motion lacks merit, it is hereby denied.", ["Inasmuch as","For the reason that","Since","All of the above are correct."], "All of the above are correct.", "All express the causal relationship between lacking merit and denial.", ["causal","denial","formal"]),
        ("Choose the correct conjunction: The penalty is imposed ___ prejudice to the filing of criminal charges.", ["with","without","notwithstanding","Both A and B could be correct depending on context."], "Both A and B could be correct depending on context.", "'Without prejudice' = criminal charges may still be filed. 'With prejudice' = they cannot. Context determines which.", ["legal expression","without/with prejudice"]),
        ("Choose the correct conjunction: ___ the respondent has been found guilty, the question now is the appropriate penalty.", ["Now that","Since","Given that","All of the above are correct."], "All of the above are correct.", "All introduce the established fact (guilt) as basis for the next issue (penalty).", ["established fact","formal","multiple correct"]),
        ("Choose the correct conjunction: The employee was dismissed not ___ he was incompetent ___ because he was dishonest.", ["because ... but","only ... also","that ... but","for ... and"], "because ... but", "'Not because...but because' — corrects the assumed reason and states the actual one.", ["not because...but because","correction"]),
        ("Choose the correct conjunction: ___ the complainant's allegations are serious, they remain unsubstantiated by competent evidence.", ["While","Although","Notwithstanding that","All of the above are correct."], "All of the above are correct.", "All introduce concessive clauses — acknowledging seriousness while noting lack of evidence.", ["concessive","multiple correct","formal"]),
        ("Choose the correct conjunction: The regulation shall remain in force ___ it is expressly repealed or amended.", ["until","unless","so long as","Both A and B are correct."], "Both A and B are correct.", "'Until' (temporal endpoint) and 'unless' (negative condition) both work for expressing continued validity.", ["until/unless","legal validity"]),
        ("Choose the correct conjunction: ___ the penalty of dismissal is the supreme penalty in administrative cases, it must be supported by substantial evidence.", ["Inasmuch as","Considering that","Given that","All of the above are correct."], "All of the above are correct.", "All express the causal reasoning — because dismissal is severe, strong evidence is needed.", ["causal","substantial evidence","formal"]),
        ("Choose the correct conjunction: The employee was found guilty of simple neglect of duty ___ of the more serious charge of gross neglect.", ["instead","rather than","and not","All of the above are correct."], "All of the above are correct.", "All express that the finding was for the lesser offense, not the greater one.", ["instead of","lesser offense","formal"]),
        ("Choose the correct conjunction: ___ the respondent's length of service is a mitigating circumstance, it does not excuse the commission of the offense.", ["While","Although","Even though","All of the above are correct."], "All of the above are correct.", "All introduce concessive clauses acknowledging mitigation while maintaining guilt.", ["concessive","mitigating circumstance","formal"]),
