        ("Identify the error: 'The employee only submitted three of the required documents.'", ["The employee submitted only three of the required documents.","Only the employee submitted three of the required documents.","The sentence is correct as written.","Both A and B are possible corrections depending on meaning."], "The employee submitted only three of the required documents.", "'Only' should be placed before 'three' to mean 'just three.' Before 'submitted' it could mean 'merely submitted.'", ["misplaced only","limiting modifier"]),
        ("Choose the sentence WITHOUT a dangling modifier:", ["Walking to the office, the rain started to pour.","Walking to the office, she got caught in the rain.","Exhausted from the long meeting, the coffee was a welcome relief.","Having been approved, the director signed the document."], "Walking to the office, she got caught in the rain.", "'Walking to the office' correctly modifies 'she' (the person walking).", ["dangling modifier","correct usage"]),
        ("Identify the squinting modifier: 'Employees who work overtime frequently receive commendations.'", ["Employees who frequently work overtime receive commendations.","Employees who work overtime receive commendations frequently.","Both A and B are valid interpretations.","The sentence is clear as written."], "Both A and B are valid interpretations.", "'Frequently' is a squinting modifier — it could modify 'work overtime' or 'receive commendations.' Repositioning clarifies.", ["squinting modifier","ambiguity"]),
        ("Choose the sentence with CORRECT modifier placement:", ["The committee nearly reviewed all 500 applications.","The committee reviewed nearly all 500 applications.","Nearly the committee reviewed all 500 applications.","The committee reviewed all nearly 500 applications."], "The committee reviewed nearly all 500 applications.", "'Nearly' should modify 'all 500' (close to all), not 'reviewed' (they did review them).", ["misplaced nearly","limiting modifier"]),
        ("Identify the dangling modifier: 'To be considered for promotion, all requirements must be submitted on time.'", ["To be considered for promotion, employees must submit all requirements on time.","All requirements must be submitted on time for promotion consideration.","Both A and B correctly fix the error.","The sentence is already correct."], "Both A and B correctly fix the error.", "'To be considered for promotion' needs a human subject (who wants promotion?). 'Requirements' cannot be considered for promotion.", ["dangling infinitive","correction"]),
        ("Choose the sentence with CORRECT modifier placement:", ["He merely suggested that the policy be revised.","He suggested merely that the policy be revised.","He suggested that the policy merely be revised.","He suggested that the policy be merely revised."], "He merely suggested that the policy be revised.", "'Merely' modifies 'suggested' — he only suggested it (didn't demand). Other placements change meaning.", ["merely placement","adverb scope"]),
        ("Identify the misplaced modifier: 'The director approved the budget for the project that was revised.'", ["The director approved the revised budget for the project.","The director approved the budget for the revised project.","The sentence is ambiguous — was the budget or the project revised?","The sentence is correct as written."], "The sentence is ambiguous — was the budget or the project revised?", "'That was revised' could modify 'budget' or 'project.' Restructuring eliminates ambiguity.", ["misplaced relative clause","ambiguity"]),
        ("Choose the sentence WITHOUT a modifier error:", ["Hoping to pass the exam, the reviewer was studied thoroughly.","Hoping to pass the exam, she studied the reviewer thoroughly.","The reviewer was studied thoroughly, hoping to pass the exam.","Studied thoroughly, she hoped to pass the exam."], "Hoping to pass the exam, she studied the reviewer thoroughly.", "'Hoping to pass' correctly modifies 'she' (the person hoping).", ["dangling modifier","correct usage"]),
